  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1115 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThe big story for this forecast occurs in the Monday to Tuesday\ntime frame with a potent storm system and attendant cold front set\nto approach and move through the region, bringing severe weather\nand heavy rain potential. A strong low level jet ahead of the\nsystem will begin to nudge into portions of eastern Oklahoma\naround daybreak Monday, with the overnight convection associated\nwith it expected to remain west of the area until the daylight\nhours. These showers and storms should move into areas west of\nHighway 75 during the morning hours, with a general decreasing\ntrend toward midday. Wind speeds and gusts will increase during\nthe morning and into the afternoon hours, with southerly gusts\naround 40 mph likely to occur. HREF probabilities of 40 mph gusts\nare fairly high in parts of northeast Oklahoma by late morning\nMonday. A Wind Advisory may be necessary for parts of the area \nduring the day Monday. There remains some uncertainty on how early\nMonday afternoon and evening thunderstorms will develop/move into\neastern Oklahoma in association with the main system and cold\nfront. In general, the continued thought is that the NBM POPs are\ntoo high, too early in the day, with late afternoon into early\nevening the more favored for arrival into the area and lingering\nacross the eastern fringes of the area past daybreak Tuesday\nmorning. The environment ahead of the front Monday afternoon into\nMonday night continues to be most supportive of wind threats\nsevere weather-wise, including tornadoes, with the overall\ninstability low enough to decrease the overall large hail threat.\nPrecipitable water values forecast to be near seasonal maxes will\nsupport a locally heavy rain and localized flash flooding threat,\nas well.\n\nThe front will move eastward through the region by late Tuesday\nafternoon, with much cooler and drier air moving in for the middle\nof the week. Another fast-moving disturbance looks to move\nthrough the region Thursday that may bring additional light rain\nshowers to the area. Uncertainty remains high overall headed into\nnext weekend, although models continue to hint at another system\nthat may impact the region for the next weekend or possibly into \nthe early part of the following week.",
